From b79628659c8e498934b5890c70ab304581aa5c7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:21:36 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] NSPredicate and NSExpression support secure coding and
 -allowEvaluation

NSPredicate and NSExpression did not adopt NSSecureCoding, so a
predicate could not be archived with requiringSecureCoding: or read
with +unarchivedObjectOfClass:fromData:error:, and -allowEvaluation,
which code portable to OS X sends to a predicate it has read, did not
exist (#766).

Both now support secure coding, as on OS X since 10.13, and a predicate
or expression read with secure coding raises
NSInternalInconsistencyException when it is evaluated, until
-allowEvaluation is sent to it; sent to a predicate, that allows its
subpredicates and expressions as well.  A copy evaluates, as on OS X,
and so does anything read without secure coding.

The unarchiver checks every object in a secure decode against the
classes allowed at that point, so each predicate and expression now
names what may be in it: expressions, subpredicates, its operator,
strings, and for a constant the classes OS X reads there without being
told (the property list classes, NSNull, NSOrderedSet, NSURL and
NSUUID), as well as anything the caller allowed.  NSCoder gains
-allowedClasses (OS X 10.13) for that, and NSNull supports secure
coding, since a comparison with nil holds one.  NSOrderedSet, NSURL and
NSUUID do not support secure coding here yet, so a constant of one of
those is refused until they do.

The objects that may not evaluate are kept in a table rather than
flagged in an instance variable, so that the public classes keep their
layout; evaluation takes the table's lock only while it holds
something.  SELF reads back as the shared expression, which is never
disabled.

Tests/base/NSPredicate/secureCoding.m checks each of these against what
Apple's Foundation does with the same calls.  Without the change 32 of
its 35 checks fail; with it they pass, and so do the NSPredicate,
NSKeyedArchiver, NSArchiver, NSArray, NSDictionary and NSSet tests.
